Grammar: E → T E′; E′ → + T E′ | ε; T → F T′; T′ → * F T′ | ε; F → ( E ) | id. **FIRST(X)** = terminals that can begin strings derived from X; **FOLLOW(A)** = terminals that can appear right after A ($ for the start symbol). A grammar is **LL(1)** if for each pair of alternatives A → α | β, FIRST(α) ∩ FIRST(β) = ∅ and, if β ⇒* ε, FIRST(α) ∩ FOLLOW(A) = ∅. Left recursion and common prefixes must be removed first.

`G = {"E": [["T", "E'"]], "E'": [["+", "T", "E'"], []],
     "T": [["F", "T'"]], "T'": [["*", "F", "T'"], []],
     "F": [["(", "E", ")"], ["id"]]}
EPS = "eps"
first = {A: set() for A in G}
follow = {A: set() for A in G}
follow["E"].add("$")

def first_of(seq):
    out = set()
    for X in seq:
        f = first[X] if X in G else {X}
        out |= f - {EPS}
        if EPS not in f:
            return out
    return out | {EPS}

changed = True
while changed:
    changed = False
    for A, prods in G.items():
        for p in prods:
            new = first_of(p)
            if not new <= first[A]:
                first[A] |= new; changed = True
            for i, X in enumerate(p):
                if X in G:
                    rest = first_of(p[i + 1:])
                    add = (rest - {EPS}) | (follow[A] if EPS in rest else set())
                    if not add <= follow[X]:
                        follow[X] |= add; changed = True

